Yeah. The Beverage Goblin has actually developed into two connected ideas:

1. Wild HAWG universe: Beverage Goblin is the recurring villain/foil—the little bastard tempting people toward energy drinks, junk beverages, excess caffeine, etc. That gives Wild HAWG an actual character to fight instead of advertising against an abstract category.
2. Beverage Goblin as its own company/IP: the earlier “Spotify for drinks” concept—people share what they’re drinking, build drink lineups, like/rate things, get recommendations/reminders, see what friends drink, eventually connect to stores, pricing, availability, and potentially a beverage marketplace.

And this thread added a useful twist: what if Beverage Goblin isn’t merely Wild HAWG’s villain? In his own universe/company, he’s the protagonist—the obsessive beverage hunter/curator who knows every drink, finds new ones, collects them, rates them, and brings people into beverage culture.

That gives you a pretty strong shared universe:

Wild HAWG: “Drink better.”
Beverage Goblin: “I WANT TO TRY EVERYTHING.”

So they’re philosophically opposed without Beverage Goblin needing to be genuinely evil. He can be the lovable chaos gremlin of the beverage world—and potentially a serious standalone consumer brand/IP rather than just an advertising character.

That distinction is important if we’re designing him at Coca-Cola-company caliber: we’re not designing a mascot for one soda. We’re potentially designing the Mickey Mouse of a beverage ecosystem.